# HTML Coding Standards

These standards are inferred from the existing implementation in `demo/pages/`. They aim to preserve clarity, consistency, and the declarative nature of the framework.

## HTML Fragment Focus

**Important**: These standards apply specifically to **HTML fragments** used within the html-next framework demo, not full HTML documents. HTML fragments are partial HTML content that gets loaded dynamically and integrated into the framework's page structure.

### What HTML Fragments Should NOT Include:

- `<!DOCTYPE>` declarations
- `<html>`, `<head>`, or `<body>` tags
- `<title>` tags (handled by the framework)
- `<meta>` tags (handled by the framework)
- `<script>` tags (use `<x-flow>` instead)
- `<style>` tags (use `sx:` attributes or framework CSS)

### What HTML Fragments Should Include:

- Framework custom elements (`<x-page>`, `<x-content>`, etc.)
- Content structure and layout
- State management components (`<x-data>`, `<x-flow>`)
- Dynamic content (`<x-fragment>`, `<x-include>`)
- Documentation and examples (`<x-markdown>`)

## Reference Files

- **demo/fragments/** contains XML files for reference only. Do not assume these patterns are supported without first verifying current implementation.
- Always check `src/components/` for actual component implementations before using patterns from reference files.

## Page Structure Pattern

Every page follows this consistent structure:

```html
<x-page>
	<x-navbar menuButtonShow="true" menuButtonTarget="sidebar"></x-navbar>

	<x-content>
		<!-- prettier-ignore -->
		<x-markdown>
			<!-- Documentation content here -->
		</x-markdown>
	</x-content>
</x-page>
```

## Custom Elements Usage

- Use `x-` prefixed custom elements for all framework components.
- Never self-close custom elements.
- Use proper indentation (tabs) for nested custom elements.
- Group related custom elements logically.

## Data and State Management

### Data Components

- Place `<x-data>` components at the top of the page before other content, just under `<x-page>` opening tag.
- Use descriptive camelCase names: `userProfile`, `dashboardConfig`, `salesData`.
- Use `defaultValue` for JSON data with proper escaping.
- Use `route` attribute for URL parameter capture.

```html
<x-data
	name="salesData"
	defaultValue='[
		{ "label": "January", "value": 100 },
		{ "label": "February", "value": 150 }
	]'
></x-data>
```

### Flow Components

- Use `<!-- prettier-ignore -->` comments to preserve formatting in `<x-flow>`.
- Place `<x-flow>` components after data definitions.
- Use descriptive camelCase keys: `updateCounter`, `pathChange`, `userLogin`.
- Keep flow logic focused and single-purpose.

### Prettier Rules for Blocks

- Always add `<!-- prettier-ignore -->` immediately before the following block tags:
  - `<x-schema>`
  - `<x-flow>`
  - `<x-markdown>`
- Rationale: these blocks often contain multi-line content where whitespace and newlines matter, and Prettier may mangle formatting. The pre-processing pipeline expects these blocks to be preserved as-authored.

```html
<x-flow key="updateCounter">
	const current = state.counter || 0; SetData('counter', current + 1);
</x-flow>
```

## State References

- Use `global_` prefix for state references in attributes: `global_userProfile`.
- Use `{{}}` syntax for state interpolation in content: `{{global_userProfile.name}}`.
- In flows, use `state.propertyName` syntax (without underscore).

## Styling Approach

### Prefer External CSS Over Inline Styles

- **Primary approach**: Use nearby CSS files for component-specific styling rather than inline `sx:` attributes
- **CSS file location**: Place component CSS in `src/components/ComponentName.css` and import in `src/index.js`
- **Inline styles**: Use `sx:` attributes only for dynamic/state-based styling that can't be handled in CSS
- **Separation of concerns**: Keep structural styling in CSS, interactive styling in `sx:` attributes

### When to Use Each Approach

**Use CSS files for:**

- Static component styling
- Layout and positioning
- Typography and spacing
- Hover states and transitions
- Component variants and themes

**Use `sx:` attributes for:**

- State-based styling (`WHEN` conditions)
- Dynamic values from state
- One-off styling that doesn't warrant a CSS rule
- Responsive breakpoints with state logic

### Examples

**Good - Using CSS for static styling:**

```html
<!-- Component CSS handles the layout -->
<x-button variant="primary" label="Save"></x-button>

<!-- CSS file: XButton.css -->
x-button[variant="primary"] { background-color: var(--palettePrimaryMain);
color: var(--palettePrimaryContrast); padding: 0.5rem 1rem; border-radius:
0.25rem; }
```

**Good - Using sx: for dynamic styling:**

```html
<!-- sx: for state-based styling -->
<x-box sx:display="WHEN global_isLoading THEN flex ELSE none">
	Loading content...
</x-box>

<!-- sx: for one-off dynamic values -->
<x-typography sx:color="var(--paletteTextPrimary)" sx:font-size="1.2rem">
	Dynamic content
</x-typography>
```

**Avoid - Overusing sx: for static styling:**

```html
<!-- Don't do this - should be in CSS -->
<x-button
	sx:background-color="var(--palettePrimaryMain)"
	sx:color="var(--palettePrimaryContrast)"
	sx:padding="0.5rem 1rem"
	sx:border-radius="0.25rem"
	label="Save"
></x-button>
```

## Styling with sx: Attributes

- Use `sx:` prefix for inline styling: `sx:display="flex"`, `sx:gap="2"`.
- Use numeric values for spacing (multiplied by 8px): `sx:p="2"` = 16px padding.
- **Colors**: Prefer CSS custom properties using root variables defined in `src/framework.css`: `sx:color="var(--palettePrimaryMain)"` or the same within CSS.
- **Short color names**: Use `red600` style colors only within `sx:` props: `sx:color="red600"`.
- **Property names**: Use kebab-case for CSS properties: `sx:background-color`, `sx:border-radius`.
- Group related styling attributes together.

### Examples

```html
<x-box sx:display="flex" sx:gap="2" sx:mb="4" sx:flex-wrap="wrap">
	Content here
</x-box>
```

## Conditional Rendering

- Use `WHEN` syntax for conditional attributes: `sx:display="WHEN global_userRole IS 'admin' THEN block ELSE none"`.
- Use `DEBUG` prefix for debug-only content: `DEBUG global_debugData`.

## Documentation Pattern

- Document component changes in `demo/pages/features.html` and `demo/pages/features/`
- Use `<x-markdown>` for all documentation content.
- Include comprehensive attribute tables with Type, Required, Default, and Description columns.
- Provide live examples and corresponding code snippets.

### Button Examples

```html
<x-button variant="primary" label="Primary Button"></x-button>
```

### Typography Examples

```html
<x-typography variant="h1">Main Heading</x-typography>
<x-typography variant="body1" sx:p="2" sx:color="var(--palettePrimaryMain)">
	Body text with styling
</x-typography>
```

### Box Layout Examples

```html
<x-box sx:display="flex" sx:gap="2" sx:mb="2">
	<x-box sx:background-color="blue600" sx:p="2" sx:color="white">Item 1</x-box>
	<x-box sx:background-color="green600" sx:p="2" sx:color="white">Item 2</x-box>
</x-box>
```

## Data Visualization

- Use `<x-viz-bar>`, `<x-viz-pie>`, `<x-viz-line>` for charts.
- Provide data through state references: `data="global_salesData"`.
- Use `sx:color` for custom colors: `sx:color="red500"` (short color names only in sx: props).

```html
<x-box sx:width="100%" sx:height="300px">
	<x-viz-bar data="global_salesData"></x-viz-bar>
</x-box>
```

## Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements for better screen reader support.
- Ensure proper heading hierarchy (`h1`, `h2`, `h3`, etc.).

## Common Patterns

### Page with Data and Flow

```html
<x-page>
	<x-data name="count" defaultValue="0"></x-data>
	<x-flow key="incrementCount">
		const current = state.counter || 0; SetData('counter', current + 1);
	</x-flow>

	<x-navbar menuButtonShow="true"></x-navbar>
	<x-content>
		<x-typography variant="h1">Counter Page</x-typography>
		<x-typography>Current count: {{global_count}}</x-typography>
		<x-button handler="incrementCount" label="Increment"></x-button>
	</x-content>
</x-page>
```

### Responsive Layout

```html
<x-box
	sx:display="flex"
	sx:flex-direction="WHEN screen.width < 768 THEN column ELSE row"
	sx:gap="3"
>
	<x-box
		sx:flex="1"
		sx:p="2"
		sx:background-color="var(--paletteBackgroundPaper)"
	>
		Item 1
	</x-box>
	<x-box
		sx:flex="1"
		sx:p="2"
		sx:background-color="var(--paletteBackgroundPaper)"
	>
		Item 2
	</x-box>
</x-box>
```

### State-based Styling

```html
<x-box
	sx:background-color="WHEN global_theme IS 'dark' THEN var(--paletteBackgroundDark) ELSE var(--paletteBackgroundPaper)"
	sx:color="WHEN global_theme IS 'dark' THEN white ELSE var(--paletteTextPrimary)"
	sx:p="4"
>
	Themed Content
</x-box>
```
